NAME
    sh - run a script: a file of shell lines

SYNOPSIS
    sh path
    name.sh

DESCRIPTION
    Runs the lines of a text file one after the other, each as if it
    had been typed at the prompt: built-ins, /bin programs, redirection
    and pipes. Each line is shown after the prompt before it runs.
    Blank lines and lines starting with # are skipped. A line ends at
    LF or CR (CR LF is fine); up to 128 characters of it are kept.
    sh is a shell built-in.

    A file whose name ends in .SH also runs when it is named as a
    command (build.sh, or build.sh: the shell upper-cases the name),
    and when a program EXECs it: that is how make runs its recipes.
    The script starts after the command that started it has ended.

    The first line that fails stops the script, and every script that
    started it: a message from the shell (what?, not found, ...) or a
    program that ends with a status other than 0 (STATUS). The shell
    then says "sh: stopped" and the prompt comes back.

    A script may start another (sh, name.sh, or a program's EXEC):
    that one runs, then the first goes on with its next line. Four
    may be running at once.

    A script is read from its sectors as it runs: a cd inside it
    changes the directory for the lines after it and for the prompt
    afterwards, and replacing the file while it runs (make writing a
    new make.sh) does not change the script that is running.

LIMITS
    A script is at most 64K. There are no variables, arguments, tests
    or loops: a line is just a command line. Each line goes to the
    console only: sh > FILE redirects nothing. pack moves files, so
    do not run pack from a script.
    Messages: "sh: too deep" (a fifth), "sh: over 64K", "sh: stopped".

EXAMPLES
    sh steps.sh            the lines of that file
    cd /src/os/commands
    make                   writes make.sh and runs it (man make)

